  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 750 PM Friday...\n\nShowers and locally severe storms have essentially moved SE of the \nregion or will so by 900 PM (Goldsboro). \n\nOtherwise, look for stratus and fog to develop overnight mainly \nafter 300 am, with low stratus expected at daybreak Saturday. \n\nShowers/storms Saturday should be south and west of our region. This \nmay continue into Sunday as weak high pressure builds over the \nregion. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected on Monday and Tuesday. Locally excessive rainfall will be \npossible.\n\nAs a mid/upper trough develops over the Upper Midwest and Great \nLakes region early next week, a backdoor cold front will approach \ncentral NC from the north on Monday, passing through the region on \nTuesday. Ahead of the front, a warm and moist air mass will prevail, \nwith highs mostly in the upper-80s to lower-90s on Monday before \nbecoming cooler (within a few degrees of normal) for Tuesday. PW \nvalues will be around 2-2.25 inches (125-150% of normal) both days \nas SW flow aloft brings Gulf moisture into the Carolinas. This \ncombined with weak to moderate instability will result in scattered \nto numerous showers and thunderstorms. This will be beneficial for \nthe ongoing drought but could bring locally excessive rainfall with \nhourly amounts of 2+ inches possible where storms merge or train. \nThus WPC has the entire region in a marginal (level 1 of 4) risk for \nexcessive rainfall both Monday and Tuesday, with a slight (level 2 \nof 4) risk over our far north.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... A CAD pattern looks to set up from mid to late next \nweek, resulting in mostly cloudy skies, patchy light rain and \ndrizzle, and below-normal high temperatures.\n\nMid/upper troughing will strengthen and build into the Eastern US \nfrom Wednesday through Friday. Meanwhile, anomalously strong surface \nhigh pressure will nose down from SE Canada and northern New England \ninto the lee side of the Appalachians, as a surface low drifts north \nin the western Atlantic, though models differ substantially on its \nplacement. This will result in robust N/NE low-level flow and a \nlikely CAD pattern across the southern Mid-Atlantic. Current \nforecast highs are in the upper-60s to upper-70s, but some raw \nguidance such as the GFS is even cooler. Forecast lows are generally \nmid-50s to mid-60s. POPs are in the slight to low chance range, and \ngiven the stable CAD regime, precipitation is more likely to be of \nthe stratiform variety (patchy light rain and drizzle) as opposed to \nconvective.",
